<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> to turn on a snowboard, shift your weight to your front foot, then roll from one edge to the other: press your toes for a toeside turn and your heels for a heelside turn. To carve, ride on a tilted edge through the whole turn so the board leaves a thin line instead of skidding, keeping your knees bent and your shoulders lined up with the board.</p></div>

<h2>Turning basics first</h2>
<p>Every snowboard turn is a switch from one edge to the other. Your toeside edge is under your toes; your heelside edge is under your heels. Before carving, you need to link smooth, skidded turns on a green or easy blue run. If you're still learning to stop and slide, start with <%= link_to "how to snowboard", guide_path("how-to-snowboard") %>.</p>

<h3>How to make a basic turn</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Stand in a relaxed stance:</strong> knees bent, weight centered, head up, looking where you want to go.</li>
  <li><strong>Shift weight to your front foot.</strong> This is what lets the nose of the board turn downhill. Leaning back makes the board hard to steer.</li>
  <li><strong>Look and rotate toward the turn.</strong> Turn your head and shoulders gently in the direction you want to go.</li>
  <li><strong>Change edges.</strong> For a toeside turn, press your toes and shins forward. For a heelside turn, lift your toes and press your heels while sitting slightly into the turn.</li>
  <li><strong>Finish across the hill</strong> to control speed, then repeat in the other direction.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Skidded turns vs carved turns</h2>
<p>In a skidded turn, the board slides sideways for part of the turn, which scrubs speed and feels stable. In a carved turn, the board rides on its edge the whole way, slicing a clean, narrow track. Carving is faster, more efficient, and feels smooth, but it needs good edge control and the right terrain.</p>

<h2>How to carve on a snowboard, step by step</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Choose a wide, smooth, groomed run</strong> that's well within your ability, with few people on it.</li>
  <li><strong>Get low.</strong> Bend your knees and ankles more than you do for skidded turns. A lower stance lets you tilt the board higher on edge.</li>
  <li><strong>Tilt the board, don't twist it.</strong> Start the turn by rolling your ankles and knees to put the board on edge, rather than swinging your shoulders to pivot it.</li>
  <li><strong>Commit to the edge.</strong> Hold the edge through the whole arc. If the tail washes out, you're probably twisting or not tilting enough.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep your shoulders aligned</strong> with the board, not rotated far ahead of it. Quiet upper body, active legs.</li>
  <li><strong>Transition smoothly.</strong> At the end of each carve, rise slightly, flatten the board briefly, and roll onto the new edge.</li>
  <li><strong>Check your tracks.</strong> Thin, pencil-like lines in the corduroy mean you're carving. A wide smear means you're still skidding.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Drills to build carving skill</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Falling leaf to traverse:</strong> practice holding one edge across the hill without skidding.</li>
  <li><strong>Garlands:</strong> ride across the slope, start a turn downhill, then return to the same edge. Repeat on both edges.</li>
  <li><strong>Hands on knees:</strong> touching your front knee on toeside turns and reaching back on heelside turns keeps you low and centered.</li>
  <li><strong>Wide, open carves:</strong> make big C-shaped turns at moderate speed before trying tighter, faster ones.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Board and setup</h2>
<p>Camber boards hold an edge well on firm snow and are popular for carving, while rocker and hybrid boards are looser and more forgiving. See <%= link_to "camber vs rocker", guide_path("camber-vs-rocker") %> and <%= link_to "how to choose a snowboard", guide_path("best-snowboard") %>. A board that's too short can feel unstable at carving speed; check the <%= link_to "snowboard size chart", snowboard_size_chart_path %>.</p>

<h2>Stay safe</h2>
<p>Carving builds speed, so stay in control and be able to stop at any time. Look uphill before starting a turn across the run, give people below you plenty of room, and carve on runs with good visibility. A lesson is the quickest way to fix habits like leaning back or over-rotating.</p>
